---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/languages/advanced/translation-versus-interpretation">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Translation and interpretation are <strong>two crafts sharing one name</strong>: written
    translation is a craft of time and revision; interpretation is a performance under real-time
    pressure. Both face the same wall — languages don't map one-to-one — and both develop
    systematic ways to decide what to sacrifice.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the craft, the performance, the untranslatables, and the machine" widget="equationStep" config={transCfg} />

  <h2>The translator's decision toolkit</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Skopos theory (Vermeer):</strong> the PURPOSE of the translation decides the strategy — a legal contract must be precise; a novel must be alive; an advert must sell. Same source text, different valid outputs.</li>
    <li><strong>Domestication vs foreignisation (Venuti):</strong> smooth into the target culture or keep the source's foreignness visible? The choice shapes how readers see the other culture — the literature lesson's comparative question, operationalised.</li>
    <li><strong>Localization:</strong> beyond translation — date formats, currencies, jokes, imagery, even colour connotations. Global products are localised, not translated (McDonald's menus per country).</li>
    <li><strong>Certification and domains:</strong> legal, medical and technical translation require domain expertise and certification — a translator without domain knowledge is a liability, in any language pair.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Vermeer (Skopos, 1978); Venuti, The Translator's Invisibility; Gile, Basic Concepts and Models for Interpreter and Translator Training.</p>
</TopicPage>
